WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.139 --> 00:00:05.019
Unreal Engine has always fundamentally been a game engine.

2
00:00:05.019 --> 00:00:07.360
However, the real-time capabilities of Unreal

3
00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:10.400
has allowed us to create more immersive experiences,

4
00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:13.560
especially as it relates to real-time experiences

5
00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:16.000
or in-the-moment experiences.

6
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:17.339
Unreal Engine not only allows

7
00:00:17.339 --> 00:00:20.219
us to build rich and immersive worlds,

8
00:00:20.219 --> 00:00:22.359
but also provides us a rich toolset

9
00:00:22.359 --> 00:00:24.359
for creating those experiences.

10
00:00:24.359 --> 00:00:25.980
On screen, you'll see a few examples

11
00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:29.120
of how Unreal allows us to build worlds, some

12
00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:31.960
of the tools at our disposal for building those worlds,

13
00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:35.359
as well as tools that allow us to ship games simultaneously

14
00:00:35.359 --> 00:00:37.710
on multiple platforms.

15
00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:40.090
Now, while this slide is an oversimplification

16
00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:42.469
of the game development process, the key principle

17
00:00:42.469 --> 00:00:45.289
to take away is that game development is typically

18
00:00:45.289 --> 00:00:47.210
a very fluid process.

19
00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:48.929
Game development often requires changes

20
00:00:48.929 --> 00:00:51.890
to be made during and even after production.

21
00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:53.329
Additionally, most modern games

22
00:00:53.329 --> 00:00:57.170
have some degree of live ops or ongoing content release,

23
00:00:57.170 --> 00:01:01.329
be they new in-game content, DLC, seasonal content,

24
00:01:01.329 --> 00:01:02.649
or patches.

25
00:01:02.649 --> 00:01:04.170
So in a nutshell, game development

26
00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:06.290
is a very fluid process and requires

27
00:01:06.290 --> 00:01:08.890
you to think more systematically about the way

28
00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:14.769
you build systems and content prior to and after release.
